<td id="S7.T3.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">15</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">83.0</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">4.9 (39.0/796.0)</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T3.1.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">20</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">85.1</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">5.2 (51.0/981.0)</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T3.1.1.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">25</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">87.2</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">7.0 (73.5/1043.0)</td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T3.1.1.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">30 (<span id="S7.T3.1.1.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ours</span>)</td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T3.1.1.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">95.7</span></td>
<td id="S7.T3.1.1.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S7.T3.1.1.7.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7.9</span> (<span id="S7.T3.1.1.7.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">99.4</span>/<span id="S7.T3.1.1.7.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1258.0</span>)</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Table 3: </span>Coverage and Novelty of the Constructed RPG over Iterations on MLKit-Py (o3-mini)</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RPG supports comprehensive and extensible functionality.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central challenge in repository-level generation is ensuring that repositories not only satisfy user-specified requirements but also extend beyond them in a coherent way. As shown in Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.2 Analysis of RPG’s Stability and Innovation Potential ‣ 7 Analysis ‣ RPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, ZeroRepo steadily increases coverage from 70.2% at 5 iterations to nearly 96% at 30, far surpassing baselines that remain below 60% (Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6 Main Results ‣ RPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). Simultaneously, it maintains meaningful novelty, reaching 8% with over 100 additional features, whereas baselines contribute fewer than 50. These results suggest that RPG functions as a persistent planning substrate, enabling repositories to achieve comprehensive coverage while supporting principled functional growth beyond reference implementations. Representative examples in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Examples of Coverage Calculation and Novelty Assessment ‣ Appendix E Experiment Results ‣ RPG: A Repository Planning Graph for Unified and Scalable Codebase Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a> further validates that RPG sustains coherence in both coverage allocation and novel feature proposals.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Analysis of Graph-Guided Localization</h3>

<figure id="S7.T4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_floatright" style="width:41%;">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
